// Chopping a break. The one-bar loop in break.wav is cut into 16 slices (s0..s15),
// one per sixteenth, so any order you write plays on the grid at your tempo.
// Change the tempo line and the break follows it.
// Render with:  niminal render examples/breakbeat.nml --out breakbeat.wav --bars 4
config { channels: stereo }
tempo 130bpm

// the loop is four beats long; `.beats(4)` speeds it up or down to last four beats at the tempo
brk = sample("break.wav").beats(4)
chops = brk.slices(16)

// the original order plays the break as recorded
straight = [s0 s1 s2 s3 s4 s5 s6 s7 s8 s9 s10 s11 s12 s13 s14 s15]

// ...and rearranged as a grid: a row per slice, a column per sixteenth. Move the x's to
// re-cut the break; rows play together, so slices can overlap. `X` is an accent and `o` a
// ghost note; to cut one slice off when another hits, add `.choke(g(s4, s12))` to the kit line
rearranged = grid {
  s0:  x... .... x... ....
  s4:  .... x... .... x..x
  s2:  ..x. ..o. ..X. ....
  s12: .... .... .... ..x.
  s9:  .... .... ...x ....
}

clip chop { instrument: chops, notes: rearranged }

chop.start
// live, swap the grid in by changing `notes: straight` to `notes: straight` and evaluating the clip again
